---
id: org.dxos.plugin.lingo
name: LingoPlugin
version: 0.1.0
---

A Composer plugin for reading in a language you are learning. Vocabulary lives
in the space as ECHO objects, collected into **word lists** and drilled with
flashcards on a Leitner schedule. The plugin's centre of gravity is the
**reading companion**: a read-only pane beside any document, email or
transcript that holds a whole-passage translation and a structural analysis of
the source, so hovering a word reveals its meaning and clicking a clause
highlights the same clause in the translation.

Analysis is a single cheap-model call that returns nested regions quoted
verbatim; character offsets are computed deterministically from those quotes,
because a model cannot count characters but can copy a phrase exactly. The
result is cached on an `Analysis` object keyed by subject and language, so
reopening a document costs a query rather than a model call.

## Extensions

The following extension dialects are used in this document.

| Term        | URI                          |
| ----------- | ---------------------------- |
| `type`      | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`      |
| `feat`      | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`      |
| `test`      | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0`      |
| `component` | `org.dxos.mdl.component@1.0` |
| `op`        | `org.dxos.mdl.op@1.1`        |
| `flow`      | `org.dxos.mdl.flow@1.0`      |
| `step`      | `org.dxos.mdl.step@1.0`      |

## Types

```mdl
type Level
  literals: A1 | A2 | B1 | B2 | C1 | C2
  desc: CEFR proficiency band.
```

```mdl
type Language
  desc: >
    A language the user is studying. `code` is the study language — the one the
    source is written in, inferred rather than chosen — and `baseCode` is the
    target the reader selects. A Spanish-speaker learning German and an
    English-speaker learning German hold two distinct objects and never share a
    word list by accident.
  fields:
    name: string                    # display name, e.g. "Japanese"
    code: string                    # BCP-47 tag of the language being studied
    baseCode?: string               # BCP-47 tag translations are rendered in
    level?: Level
    description?: string
```

```mdl
type Vocabulary
  desc: A word list, scoped to one language.
  fields:
    name?: string
    description?: string
    language: Ref<Language>
```

```mdl
type Progress
  desc: One word's Leitner schedule state.
  fields:
    box: number                     # 0..4; 0 repeats within the session
    reviews: number
    correct: number
    streak: number                  # consecutive correct; reset to zero on a miss
    reviewedAt?: string             # ISO 8601
    dueAt?: string                  # ISO 8601
```

```mdl
type Word
  desc: >
    A single vocabulary entry. Deck membership is the word's own `vocabulary`
    ref rather than an array on the deck, so an append is one write and every
    deck view is a reactive query. `language` is denormalized alongside it so
    the reader resolves a term across every deck for the language being read
    with one query.
  fields:
    term: string                    # the word as it appears in text
    translation: string
    lemma?: string                  # dictionary form, when `term` was harvested inflected
    reading?: string                # pronunciation or romanization (furigana, pinyin)
    partOfSpeech?: PartOfSpeech
    notes?: string
    examples?: string[]
    vocabulary: Ref<Vocabulary>
    language: Ref<Language>
    progress?: Progress
```

```mdl
type Range
  desc: Half-open character range `[start, end)` within one text.
  fields:
    start: number
    end: number
```

```mdl
type SegmentKind
  literals: paragraph | sentence | clause | vocab
  desc: Coarse to fine; a smaller index is a larger region.
```

```mdl
type Segment
  desc: >
    One analyzed region, carrying its extent in the source and — when the
    analysis ran over a language pair — the corresponding extent in the
    translation. The paired range is what makes cross-pane selection possible:
    selecting a clause in one pane highlights the same clause in the other
    without re-analyzing or guessing at alignment.
    Defined in `@dxos/nlp`, not this plugin.
  fields:
    id: string                      # stable within one Segmentation
    kind: SegmentKind
    parent?: string                 # enclosing segment's id
    source: Range
    target?: Range
    gloss?: string                  # a vocab segment's translation
    lemma?: string
    reading?: string
```

```mdl
type Analysis
  desc: >
    A cached structural analysis of one object's text. Persisted rather than
    recomputed because it costs a model round-trip on every open and the text
    usually has not changed; `sourceHash` is what makes that safe, since it is
    compared against the live text so a stale analysis is detected rather than
    trusted. Keyed by subject AND language — an object translated into several
    languages holds one Analysis per language.
  fields:
    subject: Ref<Unknown>           # a document, an email, a transcript
    language: Ref<Language>
    sourceHash: string
    targetHash?: string
    translation?: string            # the exact text the paired offsets point into
    segments: Segment[]
```

```mdl
type Settings
  desc: Plugin settings, persisted per profile.
  fields:
    language?: BCP-47 literal        # the language the reader translates into by default
    highlightKnownWords?: boolean
    translateUnknownWords?: boolean
```

## Components

```mdl
component ReaderArticle
  desc: >
    The reading companion, offered beside any object whose text this plugin can
    reach. Holds the whole-passage translation and drives analysis; the source
    document is never written to, so it stays owned by whichever plugin renders
    it.
  props:
    subject: Unknown                # the object being read
  state:
    languageCode?: string           # selection; falls back to Settings.language, then 'en'
    running: boolean                # translate + analyze in flight
    selected?: string               # committed segment id
  actions:
    run()                           # TranslatePassage, then AnalyzeText over the pair
    addSelection()                  # AddWord for the committed segment
    delete()                        # drop the translation and its Analysis
  layout: |
    ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │ [English ▾] [Word list ▾] [+] [✨] [⋯]       │
    ├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
    │ translated passage, segments decorated,      │
    │ hovering reveals a gloss popover             │
    └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

```mdl
component ReaderPane
  desc: >
    One read-only editor pane with the analysis revealed. Read-only by
    construction. Callbacks are held in a ref so they stay out of the extension
    identity — an extension array that changes rebuilds the editor, which would
    tear down the decorations the selection just produced.
  props:
    content: string
    side: 'source' | 'target'       # which of a segment's two ranges to decorate
    analysis?: Segmentation
    selected?: string               # externally-driven, mirroring the other pane
  emits:
    onSelect(segment?: Segment)
    onActivate(segment: Segment)
```

```mdl
component VocabularyArticle
  desc: A word list, ordered by term or by due date.
  props:
    vocabulary: Vocabulary
```

```mdl
component FlashcardsArticle
  desc: >
    Companion drill over a deck's due words. Each answer is written straight
    through RecordReview, so a session that is abandoned mid-way keeps its
    progress.
  props:
    vocabulary: Vocabulary
```

## Operations

```mdl
op AddWord
  key: org.dxos.operation.lingo.addWord
  desc: Adds a word to a deck, deduplicating on the term.
  input:
    vocabulary: Ref<Vocabulary>
    term: string
    translation: string
    lemma?: string
    reading?: string
    partOfSpeech?: PartOfSpeech
    examples?: string[]
  output:
    word: Ref<Word>
    existing: boolean               # true when the term was already present
  effects: [echo:write]
```

```mdl
op RecordReview
  key: org.dxos.operation.lingo.recordReview
  desc: Records one flashcard answer and advances the word's Leitner schedule.
  input:
    word: Ref<Word>
    correct: boolean
  output:
    progress: Progress
  effects: [echo:write]
```

```mdl
op TranslateTerm
  desc: >
    Translates a single term in context. Backs the reader's hover card for words
    no deck contains; the result is not persisted until the user adds it.
  input:
    term: string
    language: Ref<Language>
    context?: string                # the sentence the term appeared in
  output: Candidate
  effects: [ai]
```

```mdl
op TranslatePassage
  key: org.dxos.operation.lingo.translatePassage
  desc: >
    Translates a whole passage into the base language, preserving its markdown
    so the two panes line up paragraph for paragraph. Distinct from swapping
    known terms inline: grammar and word order have to move too, which only a
    passage-level pass does. The source language is inferred and returned.
  input:
    text: string
    language: Ref<Language>
  output:
    text: string
    sourceCode?: string             # BCP-47 tag detected in the source
  effects: [ai]
```

```mdl
op AnalyzeText
  key: org.dxos.operation.lingo.analyzeText
  desc: >
    Analyzes a passage into nested paragraph / sentence / clause / vocabulary
    regions and caches the result on an Analysis object. Re-running is cheap by
    design: an analysis whose hashes still match is returned as-is. Vocabulary
    is harvested from the analysis rather than by a second model call — the
    regions were chosen with the whole passage in view and already carry gloss,
    lemma and reading.
  input:
    subject: Ref<Unknown>
    text: string
    language: Ref<Language>
    translation?: string            # enables paired ranges
    vocabulary?: Ref<Vocabulary>    # where to file harvested terms; omitted, nothing is harvested
    refresh?: boolean
  output:
    analysis: Ref<Analysis>
    cached: boolean
    added: number                   # words added to the word list
  effects: [ai, echo:write]
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Vocabulary
  desc: Word lists as ECHO objects, scoped to a language.

  req F-1.1: User can create a Language and a Vocabulary via the plugin's create menu.
  req F-1.2: Deck membership is the Word's `vocabulary` ref; a deck holds no array of words.
  req F-1.3: Word.language is denormalized so a term resolves across every deck for that language.
  req F-1.4: AddWord deduplicates on the term and returns the existing Word rather than failing.
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Flashcards
  desc: Leitner drill over a deck's due words.

  req F-2.1: A word with no `progress` is treated as box 0 and is due immediately.
  req F-2.2: A correct answer advances one box; a miss resets to box 0 and zeroes the streak.
  req F-2.3: Box intervals are 0, 1, 3, 7 and 21 days.
  req F-2.4: Each answer is persisted as it is given, not batched at the end of a session.
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Reading companion
  desc: A read-only pane beside any object whose text the plugin can reach.

  req F-3.1: Offered on Markdown documents, Text objects, and any type registered under
             `AppCapabilities.TextContent` — which is how email, transcripts and magazine posts
             become readable without this plugin knowing those types exist.
  req F-3.2: The companion never writes to the object it reads.
  req F-3.3: The language selector defaults to `Settings.language`, then to English — never to
             whichever language happens to sort first.
  req F-3.4: Translating and analyzing is one explicit action; neither runs on open.
  req F-3.5: The translation and its Analysis can be deleted, returning the pane to its initial state.
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Structural selection
  desc: Hover and click select the most specific analyzed region under the pointer.

  req F-4.1: Hovering outlines the most specific region covering the position; clicking commits it.
  req F-4.2: The committed selection is distinct from the editor's own text selection.
  req F-4.3: A committed segment drives the popover, the other pane, and the toolbar actions.
  req F-4.4: Ranges are NOT mapped through document changes. A whole analysis stops being
             meaningful once the text moves, so divergence is detected by hash and the decorations
             dim until a fresh analysis arrives — mapping would keep confident-looking boundaries
             over text they no longer describe.
  req F-4.5: Deck vocabulary produces `vocab` segments too, so the editor has one selection
             mechanism rather than two; those stay deterministic and offline.
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Analysis caching
  desc: One Analysis per subject and language, reused until the text diverges.

  req F-5.1: Analysis is matched by filtering on the subject AND language refs.
  req F-5.2: A same-space Ref stores an unqualified URI, so matching compares refs rather than
             `ref.uri` against `Obj.getURI` — the string comparison never matches and every run
             created a duplicate.
  req F-5.3: A cached analysis whose `sourceHash` and `targetHash` both match is returned without
             calling the model.
  req F-5.4: `refresh` forces a fresh analysis regardless.
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
test T-1: Create a word list
  given: user is in a space in Composer
  when: user creates a Vocabulary via the plugin's create menu
  then:
    - Vocabulary object created in the space
    - Vocabulary.language is a Ref to a Language object
```

```mdl
test T-2: Leitner schedule
  given: a Word with no progress
  when: the user answers correctly three times in a row
  then:
    - progress.box is 3
    - progress.streak is 3
    - progress.dueAt is 7 days after the last answer
```

```mdl
test T-3: Leitner reset
  given: a Word in box 3 with a streak of 3
  when: the user answers incorrectly
  then:
    - progress.box is 0
    - progress.streak is 0
```

```mdl
test T-4: Most specific region wins
  given: a passage analyzed into paragraph, sentence, clause and vocab regions
  when: the pointer rests over a word inside a clause inside a sentence
  then:
    - the vocab region is outlined, not its enclosing clause
```

```mdl
test T-5: Cross-pane selection
  given: an analysis run over a source and its translation
  when: a clause is committed in the source pane
  then:
    - the paired range is highlighted in the translation pane
    - no re-analysis is performed
```

```mdl
test T-6: Analysis is reused
  given: an Analysis whose sourceHash matches the document's text
  when: AnalyzeText runs again for the same subject and language
  then:
    - output.cached is true
    - no model call is made
    - no second Analysis object is created
```

```mdl
test T-7: Stale analysis dims
  given: a document with an analysis and decorations rendered
  when: the document text is edited so its hash diverges
  then:
    - decorations render dimmed
    - ranges are not remapped onto the edited text
```

```mdl
test T-8: Harvesting from the analysis
  given: AnalyzeText is given a vocabulary ref
  when: the analysis produces vocab regions carrying glosses
  then:
    - one Word is added per region not already in the deck
    - output.added counts only the newly added words
```

## Design Notes

**The model quotes; deterministic code counts.** `segmentText` asks for regions
quoted verbatim and never for offsets. A model cannot count characters
reliably but can copy a phrase exactly, so `alignSegments` locates each quote in
the source and computes the range itself. Children are aligned within their
parent's extent, and a quote that cannot be located is dropped along with its
children rather than guessed at.

**Two hashes, not one.** A paired analysis is only valid against the exact
translation its target ranges point into. Re-translating the same source moves
those offsets, so `targetHash` is checked alongside `sourceHash`.

**Segmentation is shared, not plugin-local.** `Segment`, `Segmentation` and the
aligner live in `@dxos/nlp` beside the POS parser, so an extraction pipeline can
reuse them without depending on a UI plugin.

**Tokenization uses `Intl.Segmenter`.** A `\p{L}+` regex returns a whole
Japanese sentence as one token, so no deck term ever matched. The segmenter is
paired with a longest-match over up to four adjacent segments, so compounds ICU
splits ("パン屋" → "パン" + "屋") still resolve.

**Reading is requested only where it helps.** The analysis prompt asks for a
`reading` on every vocab region when the script needs one — furigana for
Japanese, pinyin for Mandarin — and explicitly suppresses it otherwise, since a
pronunciation guide for a script the learner already reads is noise.

## QA

Executable flows. Each step carries a human instruction (`do`) and, where the step maps to an
invocable operation, an agent binding (`invoke`). Report failures by flow and step number.

plugin-lingo is dev-only in `getDefaults` (`isDev`), so OFF by default for a normal user.

```mdl
flow QA-1: Add a word to a deck and drill it to box 3
  desc: The core vocabulary round-trip — create a Language and Vocabulary, add a Word,
    and answer it correctly three times through the Leitner schedule.
  actors: human
  covers: [F-1.1, F-1.4, F-2.1, F-2.2, F-2.3]
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open
    - collection: a collection in that space, to create into
    - plugin-lingo is enabled — it is OFF by default (isDev-only default). Enabling is a UI
      action (Settings -> plugin registry).
    - no Vocabulary named "QA Deck" exists — ABORT rather than deleting one this flow did not create
  test:
    - name: Create a Language and Vocabulary
      do: In the navtree, click + on a collection and choose Vocabulary. Name it "QA Deck" and
        create a Language "Spanish" (code "es") for it.
      capture: created
      expect: a Vocabulary named "QA Deck" appears under the collection, with Vocabulary.language
        a Ref to the new Language
      note: |
        NO `invoke` — no create operation exists for Vocabulary or Language (only op:AddWord and
        op:RecordReview are registered); the create menu is a UI factory.
    - name: Add a word
      do: In the VocabularyArticle, add the term "gato" translated as "cat".
      invoke: [op:AddWord] { vocabulary: $created.vocabulary, term: "gato", translation: "cat" }
      capture: added
      expect: "gato" appears in the word list, due immediately (box 0)
      assert: return !$added.existing;
    - name: Answer it correctly (1st)
      do: Open FlashcardsArticle for "QA Deck" and answer "gato" correctly.
      invoke: [op:RecordReview] { word: $added.word, correct: true }
      expect: the word's box advances and its streak is 1
    - name: Answer it correctly (2nd)
      do: Answer "gato" correctly again.
      invoke: [op:RecordReview] { word: $added.word, correct: true }
      expect: the word's box advances again and its streak is 2
    - name: Answer it correctly (3rd)
      do: Answer "gato" correctly a third time in a row.
      invoke: [op:RecordReview] { word: $added.word, correct: true }
      expect: after the third correct answer, the word's box is 3, streak is 3, and it is due 7
        days out
      assert: return $result.progress.box === 3 && $result.progress.streak === 3;
  after:
    - name: Delete the deck
      do: Right-click "QA Deck" in the navtree and choose Delete.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [$created.vocabulary], target: $given.collection }
      expect: the row leaves the navtree
```

```mdl
flow QA-2: Translate and analyze a passage in the reading companion
  desc: The reading-companion round-trip — open a document, run translate + analyze, and confirm
    the paired panes and cached Analysis.
  actors: human
  covers: [F-3.1, F-3.2, F-3.4, F-5.3]
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open
    - document: a Markdown document with a short non-English passage is open (e.g. "Hola, ¿cómo
        estás?")
    - text: the passage text, as it appears in the open document
    - language: an existing Language ("English", code "en") selected as the target in the
        reading companion
    - no Analysis exists for this document and "English" — the first run asserts
      `cached === false`, so a leftover Analysis from an earlier run fails it
    - plugin-lingo is enabled — it is OFF by default (isDev-only default)
  test:
    - name: Open the reading companion and run analysis
      do: Open the reading companion beside the document, pick "English" as the target language,
        and click the run action.
      invoke: [op:TranslatePassage] { text: $given.text, language: Ref.make($given.language) }
      capture: translated
      expect: a translated passage renders in the companion pane
      assert: return !!$translated.text;
    - name: Analyze the pair
      do: (the run action chains straight into analysis)
      invoke: [op:AnalyzeText] { subject: Ref.make($given.document), text: $given.text, language: Ref.make($given.language), translation: $translated.text }
      capture: analyzed
      expect: the source pane shows decorated paragraph/sentence/clause/vocab regions; hovering
        one outlines the most specific region and reveals a gloss
      assert: return !!$analyzed.analysis && $analyzed.cached === false;
      note: Coalesced with the previous step — `translated.text` is live and cannot cross a
        snippet boundary.
    - name: Re-run and confirm the cache is used
      do: Click the run action again without editing the document.
      invoke: [op:AnalyzeText] { subject: Ref.make($given.document), text: $given.text, language: Ref.make($given.language), translation: $translated.text }
      expect: the same Analysis is reused; no second model call is made
      assert: return $result.cached === true;
  after:
    - name: Delete the translation
      do: Click the delete action in the reading companion toolbar.
      expect: the pane returns to its initial (untranslated) state and the Analysis object is removed
      note: NO `invoke` — deletion is a component action (`delete()`), not a registered op.
```
